  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 109 AM CDT Sat May 30 2026\n\n00z upper-air data showed a now open 500 mb wave traversing the\nRockies this morning. This feature will continue to push \nnortheastward, becoming more diffuse into the afternoon and \nevening. A dryline will situate itself along Route 283 into the\nafternoon. Think the dryline will be farther west\nthan the latest NBM depicts and therefore POPs likely too far \neast. Have trended farther west with storm chances as a result. In\nthe moist sector, dews will be in the 60s and even perhaps low \n70s across far eastern counties is expected. In this \nenvironment, thunderstorm development is once again probable. \nGiven the meager shear, higher end severe probabilities will be \nlow, though high CAPEs will likely be realized bringing at least\nsome risk for severe storms. Similar to yesterday, a large hail\nand damaging wind threat could materialize. The SPC also has \nintroduced a conditional 2% significant tornado risk. This risk \nwould only be realized if sustained supercells are able to \nutilize every bit of perhaps a slightly backed surface wind \nenvironment just ahead of the dryline and not become outflow \ndominant too quickly. Again, this risk is very low chance.\nStorms will move out of the forecast area mid to late evening.\n\nSunday will bring a break from any precipitation chances as the\nsystem bringing Saturday's weather will depart move farther \nnorth and away from southwest Kansas. A subtle ridge manages to \nwork its way into the region, bringing very warm temperatures to\nthe forecast area. Forecast highs are expected to be above \nnormal, in the 90s, with mid to upper 90s possible across south-\ncentral Kansas.\n\nPOP and storm chances return nearly daily throughout next week\nas a meandering trough centered near Alberta and Saskatchewan\npivots about the region. The southern periphery of the flow \nwill reach southward into the central Plains Monday and \nTuesday. Additional weak height falls throughout the week could \nserve as a focus for continued storm development through the \nrest of the week. Any particular day of higher chances of \nsevere, heavy rainfall, and exact storm coverage is still a bit \nuncertain, but it appears this stormy pattern will continue.",
